================================================================================
 ABI v1 CONFORMANCE OBLIGATION 12.5: THE CONCURRENCY SUITE
 The first thing in this branch to run the codec on more than one thread.
================================================================================

CONFIGURATION (R7, R14)
  rustc 1.94.1 (e408947bf 2026-03-25), release, lto=false, PIE
  incumbent  none.  This suite has no incumbent arm and quotes no ratio against
             prost: what it measures is the core against itself under two
             arrangements of ONE mechanism, which is what R14's baseline rule is
             about and which a codec-internal comparison does not engage.
  linkage    shared library (ak-core as a cdylib), both arms, confirmed from ldd
  machine    4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, shared container
  guard      ON
  arms       one source tree, two builds:
               default                     the learned-width table PER CONTEXT,
                                           which is what ABI v1 section 6 says
               --features global-widths    ONE table for the whole process, which
                                           is what section 6 REFUSES
             Both produce correct bytes, because `Mark` carries its own width by
             value -- so the comparison isolates the table's location and nothing
             else.  Separate CARGO_TARGET_DIRs, so neither build can be the
             other's stale artifact.
  R9         4 vCPUs in a shared container is the smallest contention a shared
             cache line can have.  Every penalty below is a LOWER BOUND, and the
             throughput half of this log is the part R9's warning is about.

WHY THE OBLIGATION EXISTS, AND WHY IT HAD NOT
  Stage 4 found D16 -- `ak_call_unary` took `*mut ak_client` and mutated a shared
  `Grpc`, so two host threads calling it at once raced; it worked at 1 call in
  flight and failed outright at 8 -- BY ACCIDENT, because the RPC benchmark
  happened to ask for 8.  Nothing in any slice looks for that class on purpose.
  And the learned length-prefix width table, which ABI v1 section 6 says "lives
  in the context, never process-global", had never been touched by two threads in
  any slice of this branch.

WHY TWO SHAPES, WHICH IS THE OBLIGATION'S OWN CONDITION
  The table is indexed by SITE.  One shape exercises one set of sites, converges,
  and a per-context state defect is then invisible; two shapes make the same site
  want two different widths, which is the state a defect corrupts.  Section 2
  below carries the one-shape row beside the two-shape row so the difference is
  in the log rather than in this paragraph.

