## 4. THE POSITIVE CONTROL: the suite has to be seen failing

# Planted violation: 4 threads share ONE encode context, which ABI v1 section
# 3 makes a host-owned single-threaded thing. D16's class -- a shared mutable
# handle reached through an opaque pointer -- moved from the RPC half to the
# codec half on purpose. It is a deliberate contract violation and a data race,
# so it runs in a CHILD PROCESS and nothing downstream depends on it.

  child exit: None

  The control fires, so sections 2 and 3 are statements about the code and not
  about a check that cannot fail.

  AND THE SHAPE OF THE FAILURE IS A FINDING NOBODY ASKED FOR. The violation is
  not detected as wrong bytes: two threads resizing one length prefix index the
  encode buffer past its end, the core panics inside `Enc`, and because the
  frame it has to unwind through is an `extern "C"` entry point of the ABI
  (`ak_elem_ResultRaw`), the panic CANNOT unwind and the PROCESS ABORTS.

  Three things follow, and they are for the specification rather than for this
  slice:
   1. ABI v1 section 5's error channel covers a failure the HOST reports, through
      `ak_fail` and the generated guard. A panic inside the CORE has no channel
      at all: it reaches a `extern "C"` boundary and aborts. Every entry point
      in the codec is exposed to this, not only under a shared context.
   2. Section 3 says `ak_init` installs a panic hook. It is unbuilt (this slice
      builds it -- see the lifecycle log), and a hook changes what is PRINTED,
      not whether the abort happens. Catching the unwind at the entry point is a
      different and larger change.
   3. The context already carries a `kind` word so a wrong pointer is
      diagnosable. An owning-thread id beside it would turn this abort into
      `AK_ERR_INVALID_STATE` at the first misuse rather than at an arbitrary
      later one. That is a design question and it is raised, not taken.

