================================================================================
 WHAT THE RUNS ABOVE ESTABLISH
================================================================================

1. CORRECTNESS UNDER CONCURRENCY: CLEAN, ON BOTH ARMS

   Every encode in every section matched the single-threaded reference byte for
   byte, and every decode matched it by value:

     sequence  two shapes alternating on ONE context, one thread, rotating
               through four payloads: 40 encodes, 40 decodes, 0 wrong
     together  2, 4 and 8 threads, one context each, both shapes, phases offset
               per thread so the threads are not in lockstep:
               400 / 800 / 1600 encodes, 0 wrong, 0 decodes wrong
     and the same on the --features global-widths build, which is where a shared
     table WOULD corrupt bytes if it could.

   **The codec half has no shared mutable state, and that is now a measurement
   rather than a reading of the source.**  8 threads on 4 vCPUs is deliberate:
   oversubscription puts a preemption between a `begin` and its `end`, which is
   where a state defect that needs a window would show.

2. THE SUITE IS SEEN FAILING, AND THE SHAPE OF THE FAILURE IS THE FINDING

   The planted violation is the one a C host can make and no type system stops:
   four threads sharing ONE encode context, which ABI v1 section 3 makes a
   host-owned single-threaded thing.  D16's class, moved from the RPC half to the
   codec half on purpose.  It runs in a child process, because:

   **It is not detected as wrong bytes.  It ABORTS THE PROCESS.**  Two threads
   resizing one length prefix index the encode buffer past its end, the core
   panics inside `Enc`, and the frame the panic has to unwind through is an
   `extern "C"` entry point of the ABI (`ak_elem_ResultRaw`) -- so the unwind is
   refused and the runtime aborts:

     thread caused non-unwinding panic. aborting.

   Three consequences, all for the specification rather than for this slice:

   a. **ABI v1 section 5's error channel does not cover a panic in the core.**
      Section 5 is about a failure the HOST reports, through `ak_fail` and the
      generated guard, and that half works.  A panic INSIDE the core reaches an
      `extern "C"` boundary and aborts, and every codec entry point is exposed to
      it, not only under a shared context.  Section 5 calls the error channel
      "the widest hole in the drafted interface"; this is a second hole beside it
      and it has no channel at all.
   b. **Section 3's panic hook does not fix this.**  A hook changes what is
      printed, not whether the abort happens.  Catching the unwind at each entry
      point is a different and larger change, and it is the one that would turn
      this into `AK_ERR_PANIC`, which the error enum already reserves.
   c. **The context already carries a `kind` word** so a wrong pointer is
      diagnosable.  An owning-thread id beside it would turn this abort into
      `AK_ERR_INVALID_STATE` at the FIRST misuse rather than at an arbitrary
      later one.  Raised, not taken: it is a design question.

3. SECTION 6's "NEVER PROCESS-GLOBAL" IS NOW MEASURED ON A SECOND HOST, AND THE
   SIGN AGREES WHILE THE MAGNITUDE DOES NOT

   ns per encode, three runs of each build, ranges across runs:

   SHARED SITES (P1.1 + P1.3, both M1, so both index the same length-prefix
   sites and `ResultRaw`'s wants 2 bytes in one and 1 in the other):

     threads   per-context      process-global    ratio            ranges
     1         3459 - 3687      3200 - 3544       0.868 - 1.024    OVERLAP
     2         3514 - 3593      3610 - 3761       1.005 - 1.070    DISJOINT
     4         3611 - 3675      3875 - 4003       1.054 - 1.109    DISJOINT

   DISJOINT SITES (P1.3 + P2.5, different messages, so different sites -- but
   still the same cache lines of one 4 KB static array):

     threads   per-context      process-global    ratio            ranges
     1        10032 - 10375     9761 - 9848       0.941 - 0.982    DISJOINT
     2         9758 - 10520     9845 - 9993       0.936 - 1.024    OVERLAP
     4        10118 - 10671     9550 - 10215      0.895 - 1.010    OVERLAP

   **The global table costs 0.5 to 11 percent, only above one thread, and only
   when two shapes want different widths at the SAME SITE.**  The disjoint-site
   control shows no penalty at any thread count, so this is TRUE sharing and not
   false sharing of the static's cache lines -- which the control was added to
   separate, and which the first version of this suite could not, because it had
   only the disjoint pair and therefore found nothing.

   **The one-thread rows are the confound, and they are in the log rather than
   removed.**  The global table is 2 to 6 percent CHEAPER at one thread, because
   a static array is reached more cheaply than a `Box<[u8]>` held in the context.
   So the arms differ in access path as well as in location, and the contention
   figure above is a difference of differences.  Stated rather than corrected:
   correcting it would mean an artificial indirection nobody would ship.

   **Against the branch's inherited figure.**  The provenance table attributes
   "1.32 to 2.23 times aggregate throughput at two threads, against a global
   table" to the java slice.  Here at two threads it is 1.005 to 1.070 and it
   takes four threads to reach 1.11.  The sign agrees; the magnitude is an order
   of magnitude smaller.  R9 is the reason not to read that as a contradiction: 4
   vCPUs is a lower bound on contention, and a JVM host writing the same line
   from more cores would sit further along the same curve.

4. THE CONTENTION ARM ACTUALLY CONTENDS, AND IT IS COUNTED

   A contention arm that never contends measures nothing, so section 1b counts
   it from a counting build instead of arguing it:

     warm context                prefix misses    bytes moved
     P1.1 alone                              0              0
     P1.1 / P1.3 alternating                40           4140

   40 encodes, 40 misses: exactly one per encode.  The learned width converges
   WITHIN an encode -- the first element misses and the remaining 3 or 299 hit --
   and is then taken back by the next encode of the other shape.  That is the
   state a global table puts every thread in at once, and it is why the pair was
   chosen.  It is also a small result of its own about the mechanism: a width
   miss costs one element, not one message.

5. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH

   * **The RPC half is not in this suite.**  D16 was an RPC defect and its fix
     (the client holds the Channel, a call clones it) is exercised only by stage
     4's 8-in-flight arm, which is the accident this obligation exists to
     replace.  A concurrency suite over `ak_call_unary` with an assertion per
     response, rather than a throughput figure, is still missing.
   * **The decode context is covered only as far as the codec goes.**  The pull
     family's record buffer is per context and is exercised by the sequence
     section; it is not exercised by two threads on one context, because that is
     the violation of section 2 and it aborts.
   * **Nothing here is a race DETECTOR.**  The suite asserts outputs; it does not
     run under a sanitiser.  A defect that races without changing bytes on this
     machine at this thread count would pass.  ThreadSanitizer on the cdylib is
     the obvious next step and is not done.
   * **4 vCPUs, one container, one microarchitecture**, and the throughput half
     is visibly noisy: the per-context shared-site row moved by up to 6 percent
     between runs of the same binary.  The claim rests on the RANGES not
     overlapping at 2 and 4 threads, not on any single figure.
   * **Two shapes, as the obligation asks, and not more.**  A third shape that
     shared sites with both would be a stronger test of the same thing.
   * The `global-widths` feature is OFF by default and no other slice's build
     turns it on; the default build is byte-for-byte the code that was there
     before it existed.  It is an addition to the shared core (R0) and the
     aggregating session should say whether it stays.
